.\" lulibre(1). The guides in docs/ say more; :help inside lulibre reads them.
.TH LULIBRE 1 "October 2026" "lulibre 0.4.0-pre-alpha" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lulibre \- look after the metadata of a calibre library, from the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lulibre
.RB [ \-L
.IR library ]
.RB [ \-C
.IR confdir ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR name=value ]...
.RB [ \-\-clean ]
.RI [ command
.RI [ args ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lulibre
reads a calibre library (its
.I metadata.db
and the book files) and helps put its metadata right: it reads the first
pages of every file into a search index, with OCR where the text is not
usable, proposes titles, authors, dates and publishers from what the files
say and from online records, and lets you review and edit them.
.PP
It never writes to the library itself. Every change goes to calibre through
.BR calibredb (1),
through calibre's content server when the server has the library open, is
recorded in a journal with the value it replaced, and can be undone.
.PP
With no
.I command
it opens the interactive interface: a list of the books, a preview of the
file under the cursor, and panels for editing and review. There,
.B ?
shows the keys under headings that open and close, with a search;
.BI :help " topic"
opens the guides where they speak of a topic, as vim's help does.
.SH COMMANDS
Each takes
.B \-h
for its own options.
.TP
.BR index " [" \-\-pages " \fIN\fR] [" \-\-force "] [" \-\-retry "] [\fIfilter\fR]"
Read the first pages of every file into the search index, with OCR as the
setting
.B ocr
says. Files that have not changed are skipped, so it is cheap to run again
and safe to interrupt.
.TP
.BR query " [" \-f " \fIcols\fR] [" \-n " \fIN\fR] [" \-c "] [" \-H "] [\fIfilter\fR]"
List the books a filter matches, one to a line, tab-separated.
.B \-c
prints only how many.
.TP
.BR show " [" \-p " \fIpage\fR] [" \-l "] \fIid\fR"
One book: its metadata, its files and what reading them came to, the text
of a page.
.TP
.B stats
What is missing across the library, and what reading the files came to,
each line with the filter behind it.
.TP
.BR run " \fIfile\fR [\fIargs\fR]"
Run a Lua file without the interface.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-L " dir"
The calibre library. Otherwise the setting
.B path.library
(in
.IR init.lua ,
or the saved state), else
.BR LULIBRE_LIBRARY ,
else the current directory when it is a library; else, on a terminal,
lulibre asks whether to open the library calibre opened last (calibre's
.IR global.py.json ),
for that run.
.TP
.BI \-o " name=value"
A setting for this run. It lies over what
.I init.lua
sets as lulibre starts; a change made later replaces it.
.B "lulibre \-o help"
lists them all.
.TP
.BI \-C " dir"
The configuration directory.
.TP
.B \-\-clean
Do not load
.IR init.lua .
.TP
.B \-V
Print the version and the number of the build.
.SH FILTERS
One language everywhere a filter is taken. Terms separated by spaces must
all hold;
.B or
starts an alternative; a leading
.B \-
negates.
.PP
.nf
    word  "a phrase"            in title, authors or tags
    author:erdos  subject:number-theory  year:>1990  fmt:djvu
    title:~^%d+   field:=exact   field:^prefix   field:none
    text:"class field"          the indexed pages
    no:author  no:date  has:doi  is:marked  is:filetitle
    sort:-added                 the order
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/lulibre/init.lua
Plain Lua, run at startup as the first task; lulibre never writes into
it, and lulibre-setup writes one only where there is none. Modules of your own go in
.I lua/
beside it.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/lulibre/settings
The saved settings, read and written while the setting
.B persistent
is true.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/lulibre/
The index (one file for each library) and beside it its journal
.RI ( <index>.journal.db :
every change, where its value came from) and custom fields
.RI ( <index>.custom.db ).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LULIBRE_LIBRARY
The calibre library, when
.B \-L
is not given.
.TP
.B LULIBRE_CONFIG_DIR
The configuration directory, when
.B \-C
is not given.
.TP
.BR XDG_DATA_HOME ", " XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_STATE_HOME
Where the data, configuration and state folders are looked for.
.TP
.BR LUA_PATH_5_5 ", " LUA_CPATH_5_5
Lua's own search path, after lulibre's own modules, yours, and the setting
.BR path.modtree ;
pixi's environment sets them (with Lua 5.4, the _5_4 names).
.TP
.B PIXI_ENVIRONMENT_NAME
Set in a pixi environment, where
.B path.modtree
is ignored.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 when a command or a script fails.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR calibredb (1),
.BR calibre\-server (1)
.PP
The guides installed with lulibre (workflows, keys, filters, configuration,
api, development):
.BI ":help " topic
in the interface, or the files of
.I docs/
under its data directory.
